{title: Crown Him with Many Crowns}
{artist: Matthew Bridges (1851), Godfrey Thring (1874)}
{key: D}
{tempo: 96}
{time: 4/4}
{meta: tune DIADEMATA - George J. Elvey (1868)}
{meta: public_domain Text 1851, tune 1868: both published before 1931 (US public domain); all authors, translators and composers died over 70 years ago. Chords and section layout: original simple arrangement by Tabernacle Tools. Original verses only, no modern additions.}

{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
[D]Crown Him with [G]many [D]crowns, the [Bm]Lamb up[E]on His [A]throne;
[D]Hark! how the [G]heavenly [D]anthem drowns all [Em]music [A]but its [D]own:
A[D]wake, my soul, and sing of [G]Him who [D]died for thee,
And [G]hail Him [D]as thy [A]matchless [Bm]King through [G]all e[A]terni[D]ty.
{end_of_verse}
{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
[D]Crown Him the [G]Lord of [D]life, who [Bm]triumphed [E]o'er the [A]grave,
And [D]rose victo[G]rious [D]in the strife for [Em]those He [A]came to [D]save;
His [D]glories now we sing who [G]died, and [D]rose on high,
Who [G]died, e[D]ternal [A]life to [Bm]bring, and [G]lives that [A]death may [D]die.
{end_of_verse}
{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
[D]Crown Him the [G]Lord of [D]love; be[Bm]hold His [E]hands and [A]side,
Rich [D]wounds, yet [G]visi[D]ble above, in [Em]beauty [A]glori[D]fied:
No [D]angel in the sky can [G]fully [D]bear that sight,
But [G]downward [D]bends his [A]burning [Bm]eye at [G]myster[A]ies so [D]bright.
{end_of_verse}
